WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Do twenty answers reveal a whole school?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
First ask whom you approached.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Sampling considers the population, selection and response.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
For a school-wide question, the target is all students.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
The voices heard may cover only one part.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
A basketball court tends to reach basketball fans.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Change the entry point, and the sample may change.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Broader coverage can reduce some omissions.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Check who had a chance and who responded.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
More responses do not automatically remove selection bias.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Random sampling differs from random assignment.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
These people and votes are illustrative simulations.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
When “everyone thinks,” ask who counts as everyone.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Check recruitment and nonresponse.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Real surveys require privacy and consent.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Numbers need denominators. Voices need origins.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Leave a chance for missing voices to be heard.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Ask who before asking how many.
